"edit_calls": 1, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/step5-opus-aa-1017-omp36-aa/omp-36/cells/aa-1/trig-omp-reported/2/workspace", "first_after_build": false, "first_side": "aa-2", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/17.3.8 (/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-cache/bin/omp/5347ff91e8f44f2c69a79d7b152b7301687cd5c8/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Naive inputs have to keep working (`test_naive`, `test_day_key`), so adding `%z` alone would break them. `datetime.fromisoformat` handles both on Python \u22653.11 (this env runs 3.13).\nI fixed `parse_date` in `dates/parse.py`. It used `strptime(s, \"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S\")`, which can't read the trailing `Z`. It now uses `datetime.fromisoformat(s)`, and all 3 tests in `tests/test_parse.py` pass, including `test_dates`.\n\n- **Why not just add `%z` to the format:** that would make `strptime` require a timezone, so plain timestamps like `\"2026-01-02T03:04:05\"` would fail. `test_naive` and `test_day_key` use those. `fromisoformat` accepts both forms.\n- **Behaviour change:** a `Z` timestamp now comes back with a UTC timezone attached, while one without a timezone still comes back without one. Comparing or subtracting one kind against the other raises a `TypeError`. Nothing else in the repo calls `parse_date` besides `day_key`, so nothing is affected today.\n- **Python version:** `fromisoformat` only accepts `Z` on Python 3.11 or later. This environment runs 3.13.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": [],